<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sordello</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1200–c. 1269</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Sordello da Goito, Sordel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sordello da Goito was an Italian troubadour from near Mantua who wrote in Occitan. After an affair with Cunizza da Romano, sister of Ezzelino III, he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> for Provence and later served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou">Charles of Anjou</a>, following him to southern Italy. His most famous poem is a lament for the lord Blacatz, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a> made him a guide in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/purgatorio">Purgatorio</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sordello.jpg" alt="Sordello" width="320" height="352" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BnF_ms._12473_fol._109_-_Sordel_de_Mantoue_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
